  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+\u22ef$",
  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}\u22ef \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
